{
  "requirement": "An mdBook chapter MAY declare a named definition using a fenced `{definition}` directive. Definitions MUST use the same chapter-qualified names, identifier rules, fence rules, and nonempty-body rules as requirements and examples. Names MUST be unique across all three kinds.\n\nEach definition MUST declare exactly one nonempty `:phrase:` option before its explanatory body. The phrase MUST be interpreted as plain text. Definitions MUST NOT require their own evidence records or determinations.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met, with no unacknowledged gaps.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs) recognizes `:::{definition} name` fences. It uses the same colon-fence, fence-length and closing-fence logic, and the same `valid_name` check, as `{req}` and `{example}`. It skips code-block ranges. It does not parse option lines for definitions, so `:phrase:` stays at the start of the body.\n- `collect_definitions` (src/evidence.rs) qualifies the name with the chapter namespace through `full_requirement_name`. It stores all three kinds in one `BTreeMap`, so a name used twice is reported as `duplicate <noun> <name>` whatever the kinds. It also reports an unclosed fence and empty text. For definitions it calls `terms::declaration` and reports any error as `definition <name>: …`.\n- `terms::declaration` (src/terms.rs) requires exactly one nonempty `:phrase:` option as the first line. It rejects a missing phrase, an empty phrase, a body that is empty after trimming, and a second `:phrase:` that starts the body, including after blank lines.\n- Definitions need no evidence. `inspect_repository_with_progress` and `validate_references` filter out `DefinitionKind::Term` when checking for missing evidence, for coverage, and for matching evidence files.\n- The phrase is rendered as plain text in `replace_definitions` and `replace_references`. `escaped_phrase` escapes Markdown-significant characters and HTML.\n- Rendering functions that implement part of this requirement carry `{impl}` markers. These are `DefinitionKind::noun`, `badge`, `preprocess_book`, `replace_definitions` and `reference_roles`. I found no unmarked code implementing declaration behavior.\n\n**Tests** (tests/definitions.rs)\n- `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` covers four failure cases: body without a phrase, an empty phrase, a duplicate `:phrase:`, and a phrase with no body. It also covers a missing closing fence, and a duplicate name across kinds (`{req} x` and `{definition} x`).\n- `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` checks that a phrase containing `*alpha* & <beta>` is rendered as escaped plain text. It also checks that the `:phrase:` line is not leaked into the rendered output.\n- `fixture` and the many tests that call `validate_repository` and expect no errors show that definitions with no evidence files are valid.\n\n**Minor test gap**\nInvalid chapter-qualified names and the uniqueness check between an example and a definition are not tested for definitions specifically. They go through the same shared code paths as requirements and examples, so I did not treat this as a gap.",
